a=(L/'ATTEMPTS.md').read_text();a+='''\n## 347254: complete public-result subsumption bridge (zero credit)\n\nUse Horton–Kloster–Sullivan 1804.05966v3, graph definition at 282–288, Theorem 4.1/Corollary 4.2 at 408–419, Appendix B proof at 876–1188. Choose an integer c≥max(C,3), where C is the theorem's existential threshold, and put m=c+1. Let the vertices be an independent set U={u1,…,uc} and m disjoint cliques Cq={vq1,…,vqc}. Inside each Cq join every distinct pair; join ui to vqi for each q,i, and add no other edges. This is a finite simple undirected connected graph. The permutations of the coordinate indices, simultaneously in U and every clique, and the permutations of the cliques act transitively on U and on its complement separately. Thus there are at most two exponential-diagonal values. Every ui has degree m=c+1 and every vqi has degree (c−1)+1=c. Hence (A²)ui,ui=c+1 and (A²)vqi,vqi=c, so the graph is NOT walk-regular. Theorem 4.1 supplies β in (0,1/(c−2)) making the two walk-class exponential-diagonal values identical. Transitivity then makes ALL vertex β-subgraph centralities identical. β>0 and all literal graph hypotheses hold; the proposed implication to walk-regularity is false. This is the exact unrestricted-β assertion, not an inference from closeness/betweenness centrality counterexamples.\n\nThe proof passage was read through its continuity step, explicit spectral formulas and its two eventually strict inequalities (limits e+1>e and e−2>1/2, with final vanishing remainder). Extracted equations have typography artifacts; no new audit or alternative proof of that public spectral theorem is claimed. Its public 2019 v3 predates the 2020 source and 2026 MathDB record; the 2021 source itself also explicitly recalls this refutation at 113–120. Journal 2019 full body/corrections were not inspected; that limits literature completeness, but the actual preprint theorem with this exact construction already supplies the subsumption bridge.\n''';write('ATTEMPTS.md',a)
